    pharm: "1. Oxygen via Venturi mask 24-28% to target SpO2 88-92% (caution: CO2 retention). 2. Nebulised Salbutamol 5mg + Ipratropium 0.5mg 4-hourly. 3. IV Hydrocortisone 200mg stat then oral Prednisolone 30mg daily for 5-7 days. 4. Antibiotics: Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 625mg PO TID for 7 days (or Doxycycline 100mg BID if penicillin-allergic). 5. Consider NIV if pH <7.35 and pCO2 >45 despite medical therapy.",
    nonPharm: "Smoking cessation counselling. Chest physiotherapy and sputum clearance techniques. Pulmonary rehabilitation referral. Nutritional support.",
    carePlan: "Admit to medical ward. Controlled oxygen therapy. Bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics as above. Monitor ABGs. Assess for NIV need. Once stable, optimise maintenance therapy: LAMA/LABA/ICS triple therapy. Vaccinate against influenza and pneumococcus before discharge.",
    dtps: "1. Acute infective exacerbation requiring immediate antibiotics and bronchodilators. 2. Risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure requiring NIV. 3. Suboptimal maintenance therapy (no ICS/LABA).",
    monitoring: "ABGs daily or as clinically indicated. SpO2 continuously. Sputum colour and volume. Temperature. Monitor for CO2 retention (headache, confusion, flapping tremor).",
    counselling: "Emphasise smoking cessation as the single most important intervention. Demonstrate correct inhaler technique. Teach sputum clearing techniques (huff cough). Explain signs of worsening to watch for. Importance of pulmonary rehabilitation.",
    followUp: "COPD clinic in 2 weeks. Pulmonary rehabilitation referral. Smoking cessation clinic. Vaccinations: annual influenza, pneumococcal (PCV13 then PPSV23).",
    pearls: "In COPD exacerbations, oxygen must be titrated carefully to avoid worsening hypercapnia by blunting hypoxic drive. Target SpO2 88-92%, not higher. NIV is first-line for hypercapnic respiratory failure. Triple therapy (ICS/LAMA/LABA) reduces exacerbation frequency in GOLD Group E.",
