    title: "New Diagnosis of Pulmonary Tuberculosis in a Young Adult",
    difficulty: "Intermediate",
    demographics: "Charles Munyao, 25-year-old male, 80 kg, Police Officer from Murang'a",
    chiefComplaint: "Chronic cough for 3 weeks, night sweats, weight loss, and haemoptysis",
    hpi: "Charles Munyao, a 25-year-old Police Officer from Murang'a, presents with a 3-week history of persistent cough producing yellowish sputum, intermittent haemoptysis (streaks of blood), drenching night sweats, unintentional weight loss of 6 kg over 2 months, and low-grade fever. Lives in a crowded shared apartment with 4 other family members. Works in a market where there is high dust exposure.",
    pmh: "No significant PMH. No prior TB treatment. No known HIV status.",
    medHx: "No regular medications",
    allergies: "Penicillin (rash)",
    pe: "Thin-built, pale conjunctivae. Respiratory: dullness to percussion over right upper lobe, bronchial breath sounds, crackles. No lymphadenopathy. No neck stiffness. No BCG scar visible.",
    vitals: "HR: 113 bpm, BP: 159/64 mmHg, RR: 24 breaths/min, Temp: 37.5°C, SpO2: 88%",
    labs: "Sputum Xpert MTB/RIF: Detected, Rifampicin resistance: Not detected. Sputum smear microscopy: AFB 2+. HIV test: Negative. Hb 10.2 g/dL. ESR 85 mm/hr.",
    imaging: "Chest X-ray: Right upper lobe consolidation with cavitation, hilar lymphadenopathy. Findings suggestive of active pulmonary TB.",
    diagnosis: "Pulmonary Tuberculosis (New, Smear-Positive, Rifampicin-Sensitive)",
    ddx: ["Pulmonary TB", "Community-Acquired Pneumonia", "Lung Cancer", "Bronchiectasis", "Fungal Pneumonia"],
    goals: "Achieve bacteriological cure, prevent transmission, prevent drug resistance, minimise drug side effects",
    pharm: "1. Start standard 4-drug intensive phase: Rifampicin 600mg daily (10mg/kg), Isoniazid 300mg daily (5mg/kg), Pyrazinamide 1500mg daily (25mg/kg), Ethambutol 800mg daily (15mg/kg) for 2 months. 2. Continuation phase (after 2 months): Rifampicin 600mg + Isoniazid 300mg daily for 4 months. 3. Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) 25mg daily with INH to prevent peripheral neuropathy. Weight-based dosing with current weight: 56 kg.",
    nonPharm: "Cough etiquette: wear mask, cover mouth when coughing, sputum disposal in designated container. Adequate ventilation at home. Nutritional counselling: high-protein, high-energy diet.",
    carePlan: "Initiate DOT (Directly Observed Therapy) through local TB clinic. Register patient in the National TB Program. Monitor for drug-induced hepatitis (check LFTs at baseline and monthly). Provide Pyridoxine. Contact tracing: screen all household contacts for TB. Test for HIV if not already done.",
    dtps: "1. Active infectious pulmonary TB requiring immediate initiation of anti-TB therapy. 2. Risk of transmission to close contacts (crowded living conditions). 3. Potential drug-induced hepatotoxicity requiring monitoring.",
    monitoring: "Weekly: sputum smear microscopy until conversion (typically 2-8 weeks). Monthly: LFTs, serum creatinine, uric acid (if on PZA). Visual acuity testing for Ethambutol toxicity (monthly). Watch for: jaundice, visual changes, rash, peripheral neuropathy.",
    counselling: "Explain the full TB treatment duration (6 months minimum). Importance of adherence — stopping early leads to drug resistance and relapse. DOT ensures compliance. Cover cough with tissue/mask. Separate sleeping area if possible. Side effects: reddish-orange urine (rifampicin — harmless), hepatitis signs (jaundice, dark urine, nausea — report immediately).",
    followUp: "TB clinic follow-up at 2, 4, and 6 months. Repeat chest X-ray at 6 months. Sputum conversion check at 2 months. If positive at 2 months, extend intensive phase to 3 months.",
    pearls: "TB is curable with standard 6-month regimens. DOT is critical for adherence. INH-induced peripheral neuropathy is prevented by Pyridoxine 25mg daily. Ethambutol can cause optic neuritis — test red-green colour discrimination monthly. Rifampicin is a potent CYP450 inducer — check drug interactions (OCPs, warfarin, ARVs).",
    references: ["Kenya National TB Program Guidelines", "WHO Guidelines for Treatment of Drug-Susceptible TB", "National TB & Leprosy Program (NTLP-Kenya)"],
