    nonPharm: "Cough etiquette: wear mask, cover mouth when coughing, sputum disposal in designated container. Adequate ventilation at home. Nutritional counselling: high-protein, high-energy diet.",
    carePlan: "Initiate DOT (Directly Observed Therapy) through local TB clinic. Register patient in the National TB Program. Monitor for drug-induced hepatitis (check LFTs at baseline and monthly). Provide Pyridoxine. Contact tracing: screen all household contacts for TB. Test for HIV if not already done.",
    dtps: "1. Active infectious pulmonary TB requiring immediate initiation of anti-TB therapy. 2. Risk of transmission to close contacts (crowded living conditions). 3. Potential drug-induced hepatotoxicity requiring monitoring.",
    monitoring: "Weekly: sputum smear microscopy until conversion (typically 2-8 weeks). Monthly: LFTs, serum creatinine, uric acid (if on PZA). Visual acuity testing for Ethambutol toxicity (monthly). Watch for: jaundice, visual changes, rash, peripheral neuropathy.",
    counselling: "Explain the full TB treatment duration (6 months minimum). Importance of adherence — stopping early leads to drug resistance and relapse. DOT ensures compliance. Cover cough with tissue/mask. Separate sleeping area if possible. Side effects: reddish-orange urine (rifampicin — harmless), hepatitis signs (jaundice, dark urine, nausea — report immediately).",
    followUp: "TB clinic follow-up at 2, 4, and 6 months. Repeat chest X-ray at 6 months. Sputum conversion check at 2 months. If positive at 2 months, extend intensive phase to 3 months.",
    pearls: "TB is curable with standard 6-month regimens. DOT is critical for adherence. INH-induced peripheral neuropathy is prevented by Pyridoxine 25mg daily. Ethambutol can cause optic neuritis — test red-green colour discrimination monthly. Rifampicin is a potent CYP450 inducer — check drug interactions (OCPs, warfarin, ARVs).",
    references: ["Kenya National TB Program Guidelines", "WHO Guidelines for Treatment of Drug-Susceptible TB", "National TB & Leprosy Program (NTLP-Kenya)"],
