    hpi: "Mercy Njoroge, a 45-year-old Farmer from Busia, presents with persistent symptoms of nasal congestion, clear rhinorrhoea, paroxysmal sneezing (especially in the morning), itchy nose and palate, and watery itchy eyes. Symptoms are worse during the rainy season but present year-round. Reports fatigue and difficulty concentrating at work due to poor sleep from nasal obstruction. Has tried various over-the-counter antihistamines with partial relief.",
    pmh: "Mild asthma (diagnosed in childhood, now well-controlled). No known drug allergies.. Also has Dyslipidaemia.",
    medHx: "Loratadine 10mg daily (intermittent use), Salbutamol MDI PRN (rarely used)",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Nasal mucosa: pale, boggy, oedematous turbinates with clear discharge. Allergic shiner (dark circles under eyes). Nasal crease (from frequent nose rubbing). Conjunctival injection. Post-nasal drip visible on oropharynx. Lungs clear.",
    vitals: "HR: 93 bpm, BP: 159/95 mmHg, RR: 25 breaths/min, Temp: 38.7°C, SpO2: 93%",
    labs: "Allergy skin prick test: Positive to dust mites (Dermatophagoides pteronyssinus), grass pollen, cockroach. Total IgE: 250 IU/mL (elevated). Eosinophil count: 6%. Nasal endoscopy: bilateral oedematous turbinates, no polyps.",
    imaging: "No imaging indicated.",
    diagnosis: "Persistent Moderate-to-Severe Allergic Rhinitis (ARIA Classification)",
    ddx: ["Allergic Rhinitis", "Non-Allergic Rhinitis", "Chronic Sinusitis", "Nasal Polyps", "Deviated Nasal Septum"],
    goals: "Control symptoms, improve quality of life and sleep, prevent complications (sinusitis, asthma exacerbation)",
    pharm: "1. Step-up therapy per ARIA guidelines: Step 1: Intranasal Corticosteroid (Fluticasone Furoate 27.5mcg, 2 sprays each nostril daily). Step 2: Add oral antihistamine (Cetirizine 10mg or Levocetirizine 5mg daily). Step 3: If eye symptoms prominent, add Olopatadine eye drops 0.1% BID. 2. Consider Montelukast 10mg daily if concomitant asthma. 3. Saline nasal irrigation daily. 4. Allergen avoidance measures.",
    nonPharm: "Allergen mitigation: dust mite covers for mattress and pillows, wash bedding in hot water weekly, remove carpets and stuffed toys, keep windows closed during pollen season, use HEPA air purifier.",
    carePlan: "Start intranasal corticosteroid daily — takes 1-2 weeks for full effect. Add oral antihistamine for immediate relief. Saline nasal rinses BID. Review in 4 weeks. If inadequate response, consider allergy immunotherapy referral.",
    dtps: "1. Chronic allergic inflammation requiring consistent anti-inflammatory therapy. 2. Impaired quality of life due to poorly controlled symptoms.",
    monitoring: "Symptom diary (nasal congestion, sneezing, rhinorrhoea, itching — 0-3 scale). Peak nasal inspiratory flow if available. Assess asthma control (ACT score).",
    counselling: "Intranasal steroids are safe and effective but must be used daily — not PRN. Correct technique: aim spray slightly away from septum. Clean nozzle weekly. Avoid decongestant sprays (risk of rhinitis medicamentosa).",
    followUp: "Allergy clinic in 4 weeks for treatment response assessment. Consider referral for immunotherapy if moderate-severe and not controlled on pharmacotherapy.",
    pearls: "Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective treatment for allergic rhinitis — they work better than oral antihistamines alone. The ARIA guidelines recommend a step-up approach. Decongestant nasal sprays should not be used for >5 days due to rebound congestion. Allergen immunotherapy is disease-modifying and should be considered early.",
    references: ["ARIA Guidelines 2024: Allergic Rhinitis", "Kenya ENT Society Guidelines", "WHO: Allergic Rhinitis Management"],
