    demographics: "Alex Njeri, 84-year-old male, 93 kg, Security Guard from Siaya",
    chiefComplaint: "Persistent cough and fever for 5 days after a common cold",
    hpi: "Alex Njeri, a 84-year-old Security Guard from Siaya, presents with a persistent hacking cough, initially dry now productive of clear sputum, associated with low-grade fever, sore throat, and general malaise. Symptoms started 5 days ago with coryzal symptoms (runny nose, sneezing). Cough is worse at night interfering with sleep. No shortness of breath, no wheezing, no haemoptysis.",
    pmh: "No significant PMH. Non-smoker. No asthma or allergies. Fully vaccinated.. Also has Hypertension.",
    medHx: "No regular medications. Has been taking over-the-counter Paracetamol and Vitamin C.",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Afebrile (37.2°C). Mild pharyngeal erythema. Lungs clear to auscultation, no wheeze or crackles. Chest clear on percussion. No lymphadenopathy.",
    vitals: "HR: 109 bpm, BP: 99/91 mmHg, RR: 24 breaths/min, Temp: 37.6°C, SpO2: 88%",
    labs: "WBC 8.2 x10^9/L (normal differential). CRP 15 mg/L. Viral PCR (nasopharyngeal swab): pending. Rapid strep test: negative.",
    imaging: "Chest X-ray (if indicated): No consolidation, no infiltrates, normal lung fields.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Bronchitis (Likely Viral Aetiology)",
    ddx: ["Acute Bronchitis", "Community-Acquired Pneumonia", "Post-Viral Cough", "Pertussis", "Asthma Exacerbation"],
    goals: "Symptom relief, exclude pneumonia, avoid unnecessary antibiotic use, return to normal activity",
    pharm: "1. No antibiotics indicated — this is almost certainly viral. 2. Symptomatic management: Honey 10mL at bedtime (cough suppressant, evidence-supported). Steam inhalation. 3. If cough severely disrupting sleep: Short-term Dextromethorphan 15-30mg at bedtime (weak evidence, use sparingly). 4. Paracetamol 1g QID PRN for fever/malaise. 5. Salbutamol inhaler only if wheeze present.",
    nonPharm: "Adequate fluid intake (warm honey-lemon drinks). Rest. Humidify room air. Avoid smoke and irritants. Sleeping with head elevated may reduce nighttime cough.",
    carePlan: "Outpatient management. Reassure patient that viral bronchitis typically lasts 7-21 days and antibiotics will not help. Return if develops high fever, shortness of breath, haemoptysis, or no improvement after 3 weeks.",
    dtps: "1. No drug therapy problem — antibiotic stewardship: viral infection not requiring antimicrobials. 2. Patient education: managing expectations about duration of viral cough.",
    monitoring: "Symptom diary. Temperature. Return if worsening symptoms or persistent fever beyond 7 days.",
    counselling: "Explain that antibiotics do not work for viral infections and taking them unnecessarily contributes to antibiotic resistance. The cough can last 2-3 weeks even as you improve. Honey at bedtime is as effective as over-the-counter cough medicines for nighttime symptoms.",
    followUp: "No specific follow-up needed. Return if symptoms persist beyond 3 weeks or worsen. Yearly influenza vaccination recommended.",
    pearls: "Acute bronchitis is one of the most common conditions leading to inappropriate antibiotic prescribing. The DECLINE trial showed that a C-reactive Protein point-of-care test reduces antibiotic prescribing safely. Honey is more effective than diphenhydramine and dextromethorphan for nocturnal cough in adults. Pertussis should be considered in adults with paroxysmal cough and post-tussive vomiting.",
    references: ["NICE Guideline: Cough (Acute)", "CDC: Management of Acute Bronchitis", "WHO: Antibiotic Stewardship in Respiratory Infections"],
